# Rust
/target/
**/*.rs.bk
Cargo.lock.orig

# Env / secrets
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# kafkaman config — rendered per env by CI/CD with injected secrets; never commit
# the resolved file. The example (dummy values, documents the keys) stays tracked.
kafkaman.toml
!kafkaman.example.toml
# The example services' configs are tracked too. They carry no secrets — the
# connection string and broker list are environment variables, not kafkaman keys
# — and they are loaded by `Config::discover()` in tests, which is the only thing
# that keeps a config file from rotting. A missing one is exactly the bug that
# left the previous example unable to start.
!examples/*/kafkaman.toml

# OS / editor
.DS_Store
*.swp

# LLM Wiki per-machine runtime/install state (absolute paths, install hashes)
.llm_wiki/runtime.toml

# Local-only raw research copies — large or private source dumps kept on disk
# for reference but intentionally not committed. The distilled summary,
# manifest, and small web/crate sources stay tracked.
raw/research/2026-06-20-kafkaman-objectives-rust-microservice-architecture/sources/01-cqrs-fullstack/

# Per-machine agent scaffolding. These hold absolute paths, install hashes, and
# per-developer tool preferences, so committing one makes the next person's
# working tree dirty on first run for a file neither of them meant to share.
#
# `.serena/project.yml` and `.serena/.gitignore` predate this rule and stay
# tracked — git keeps tracking a file the ignore list gains later — which is
# deliberate: the project id in there is shared, not per-machine.
.serena
.claude/
